WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
WESLEY CHUN: Hi, developers,
and welcome to "Serverless

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.590
Migration Station," where we
show you one serverless compute

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.073
modernization technique.

00:00:06.073 --> 00:00:07.740
I'm Wesley from Google,
joined by Martin

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.300
and our friend Porter
here to discuss

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.310
how and when you would
move an App Engine

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.473
app to Cloud Functions.

00:00:12.473 --> 00:00:13.890
MARTIN OMANDER:
That sounds great.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.110
But what I want to
know is why we'd

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.128
want to migrate an App Engine
app to Cloud Functions.

00:00:19.128 --> 00:00:20.670
WESLEY CHUN: Very
fair of you to ask,

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.800
because you want to know
whether it's worth doing.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.900
One key reason is that
you didn't have much code

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.840
to begin with, but App Engine
was all we had available.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.690
Other reasons include less
configuration, more robust

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.700
local development
experience, and the ability

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.687
to do event-based
background processing.

00:00:34.687 --> 00:00:35.520
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.550
I didn't even know you could
do this with Cloud Functions.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.638
But isn't the App Engine really
different from Cloud Functions?

00:00:41.638 --> 00:00:43.680
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah, it
seems that way, doesn't it?

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.900
App Engine is great
for full-stack web apps

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.850
or mobile backends,
while Cloud Function

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.730
sounds like it's for
event-driven microservices

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.300
or one-off utility
functions, right?

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.300
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
that's exactly right, Wes.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.570
So I suppose answering when
you'd move from App Engine

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.120
to Cloud Functions is the
first order of business

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.910
before discussing how to do it.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:03.930
WESLEY CHUN: Absolutely.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.060
Any time you're considering
switching products,

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.123
you don't do it lightly.

00:01:07.123 --> 00:01:08.790
I already mentioned
some of the benefits

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:09.998
you get with Cloud Functions.

00:01:09.998 --> 00:01:11.530
But, for something
more specific,

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.800
I'd say we take a
quick step back.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.490
There was a time
not too long ago

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.880
when App Engine was the
only serverless platform

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.820
available so you'd use it
for everything, including

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:24.090
small, single-functionality
apps, if you call them that.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.480
Short apps with not much code
are perfect for this migration.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.310
Cloud Functions has
much less overhead,

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.280
meaning you can drop app.yaml
and other configuration

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.440
files, keeping just
your app files along

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.630
with third-party
package dependencies.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:37.770
Oh, and one other thing.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.990
Cloud Functions does
not support Python 2,

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.600
so this is a great chance
to upgrade to Python 3.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.430
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah,
that makes sense.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.390
A small app like that is really
just a single microservice,

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.370
perfect for Cloud Functions.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:50.880
Cool.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.160
So what was the other
situation for considering

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.703
moving from App Engine
to Cloud Functions?

00:01:55.703 --> 00:01:57.870
WESLEY CHUN: Well, you said
the magic word, Martin--

00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:58.980
microservice.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.860
In this other scenario, you have
a huge, monolithic app made up

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.600
of mostly independent
routes which

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.300
could be almost a
collection of API endpoints.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.910
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, so you
mean I can split out my user

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.030
authentication code and
admin-only utilities

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.408
to be separate functions,
away from the main app?

00:02:15.408 --> 00:02:16.950
WESLEY CHUN: That's
right, especially

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.533
if you have different
developers doing

00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:20.890
the auth code, the database
code, the admin code, and so

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:21.390
on.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.940
Your dev team won't be
stepping on each other's shoes.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.370
Breaking up that app
into multiple independent

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.140
microservices, like
what you suggested,

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.840
is a prime candidate to
move to Cloud Functions.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.940
Better yet, if you make
them plug and play,

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.240
you can reuse those pieces
for other apps, saving time

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.800
and improve maintainability.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.410
It's a valuable refactor
in many respects.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:41.640
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds good.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.820
But what if my monolithic
application is huge

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.370
and I have a ton
of microservices?

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.970
Would that still be a good
use case for Cloud Functions?

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.720
WESLEY CHUN: It's a
good question, Martin.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.630
The general rule of thumb
for using Cloud Functions

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.870
is when you have a few or
a handful of microservices.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:59.610
If you've got many
more than that,

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.920
or there are even
dependencies between them,

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.080
you may want to consider
keeping it running as-is

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.350
on App Engine for now or
consider moving to Cloud Run,

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.630
especially if your team
has added containerization

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.673
as part of your software
development workflow.

00:03:11.673 --> 00:03:12.840
MARTIN OMANDER: Makes sense.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.360
And, if that's the case,
we've already made some videos

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.590
showing users how to migrate
from App Engine to Cloud Run,

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.787
both with and without
Docker, right?

00:03:21.787 --> 00:03:22.870
WESLEY CHUN: That's right.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.960
And both of those videos
also come with Codelabs

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.040
so you get that
hands-on experience.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.310
The rest of this video will
be for those considering

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:30.870
Cloud Functions instead.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:32.280
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds good.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.020
Can you tell us more
about the app we're

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:35.370
migrating to Cloud Functions?

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:38.100
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah, the Cloud
NDB sample app from module 2

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.170
is a great example for
us to use because it

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.720
doesn't rely on any App
Engine-bundled services.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.890
We moved it from App Engine NDB
to Cloud NDB back in module 2.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.560
And since Cloud Functions
only supports Python 3,

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.690
we're going to start
with the Module2b folder

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.600
and migrate that app
to Cloud Functions.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:55.740
MARTIN OMANDER: I see.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.800
Can you point me to the
repo, as well as the Codelab,

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.220
to remind me what we
did back in module 2?

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.220
WESLEY CHUN: Sure thing.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.110
Here are the links to
both the starting repo,

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.670
as well as the
Codelab, to remind you

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.770
of how we got there
from module 1.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.590
If you did module 2 and
ported it to Python 3,

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:11.670
start with your version.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:14.310
But if you don't have it, clone
the repo or download the ZIP

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.892
file and grab it from
the Module2b folder.

00:04:16.892 --> 00:04:17.850
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.160
Now I understand that I should
use Cloud Functions when

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.590
I have smaller bits of
code, like a utility

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:26.610
function or a microservice,
rather than a full app.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:30.240
Is there anything else to know
when getting ready to migrate?

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:30.990
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:31.980
Well, think about this.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.630
If you don't have
a full app, you

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.090
won't need a full web
framework, either, right?

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:38.820
Yes, you still have to
get, service, and respond

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.560
to requests, but that's it.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.090
You can just write
code and not have

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.920
to think about stacks
or frameworks with that.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.760
So let's go to the computer and
move this to Cloud Functions

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.485
now.

00:04:49.485 --> 00:04:51.710
You know the pattern
by now, right?

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:55.310
Deploy that app one more time to
App Engine to ensure it works.

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:57.710
This way, there's no guessing
if you run into issues

00:04:57.710 --> 00:04:58.970
after migrating.

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:02.360
From your Module2b,
run gcloud app deploy

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.600
and confirm that your app adds
a new visit and displays the 10

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.540
most recent visits, just
like it's always done.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:09.230
OK.

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:12.110
Just like when we migrated
from App Engine to Cloud Run,

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:13.495
it's a whole new platform.

00:05:13.495 --> 00:05:17.370
So I'll make an entire copy
of your module2b folder.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.460
Now let's start cleaning house.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:22.490
Delete app.yaml, an
App Engine artifact.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.000
The only config file needed is
the package requirements file,

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.262
requirements.txt.

00:05:28.262 --> 00:05:31.700
Neither that nor the HTML
index file in templates

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:33.290
require editing.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:35.760
All of the work is in the
main application file.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:37.610
And when I say work,
it sounds like a lot.

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:40.820
But it's really just
removing or tweaking things.

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:43.010
As mentioned earlier, we
don't need a web framework,

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:45.500
so strip out almost
everything from Flask.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:47.870
The app still renders
a template of visits,

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:50.060
so we do need to keep that.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:53.630
Cloud Functions will give us
a Flask request object when

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:55.970
the Cloud Functions is
called, so we don't need

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:57.980
to ask the framework for it.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:05:59.900
We removed importing
Flask, so there's

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.490
no need to instantiate it.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:03.590
No, there's no
longer an app object.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:05.240
If your app is
dependent on it, you

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:07.250
may be better off
staying with App Engine

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:09.860
or containerizing
it for Cloud Run.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:12.410
No app means we should
remove the route, as well.

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:14.840
While route is an adequate
name for the route

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:18.020
function of an app, this app is
really just a single function--

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:18.830
visit me.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:21.830
Since it's now a function, let's
give it an appropriate function

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:23.420
name, visitme().

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:25.820
Cloud Functions will call
it directly with that name.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:27.230
Plus, it's more descriptive.

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:28.790
And boom, that's it.

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:31.010
Your App Engine app is
now a Cloud Function.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:34.520
Instead of gcloud app deploy,
it's gcloud functions deploy.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:36.350
But because Cloud
Functions doesn't

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:38.360
have a config file
like app.yaml,

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:40.280
you need to specify
other aspects,

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.870
like which runtime,
the trigger type,

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.560
whether to allow unauthenticated
traffic, and possibly

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:46.882
what region.

00:06:46.882 --> 00:06:49.340
Now as you wrap up, let's chat
about some things that Cloud

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:51.350
Functions can do that
App Engine can't.

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:53.090
One key feature
is that, while you

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:55.250
can develop Cloud Functions
on the command line

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:58.160
like App Engine, you can also
do everything in the browser--

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.610
create functions, code them,
deploy them, and test them--

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:03.230
all from the Cloud Console.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.707
Pretty cool, right, Martin?

00:07:06.707 --> 00:07:07.540
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.430
That is really cool.

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:11.050
Sometimes I'm on
the road and really

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:12.790
only have browser access.

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:14.890
It's good to know that
I'll be able to make fixes

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:17.570
from my hotel room if needed.

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:19.960
I think there's another
thing Cloud Functions can do

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.702
that App Engine can't, right?

00:07:22.702 --> 00:07:24.160
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah,
and perhaps this

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:25.327
is a product differentiator.

00:07:25.327 --> 00:07:28.150
We know App Engine apps
are HTTP-triggered.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:30.010
Well, Cloud Function
supports that.

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:31.750
But now we can
execute our function

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.390
in the background as part
of an event-driven process.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:36.580
Cloud Functions can be
triggered if someone uploads

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.560
a file to Cloud
Storage, a new message

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:40.270
comes into Cloud
Pub/Sub, someone

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:42.280
logs into Firebase, and so on.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:43.270
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:44.890
It's cool that
event-driven means

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:47.230
you can execute different
Cloud Functions as part

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:49.762
of one single workflow.

00:07:49.762 --> 00:07:51.470
Now you mentioned we
can develop, deploy,

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:52.840
and test in the browser.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:55.480
But I'm really an
old-school developer.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.630
What if I want to do everything
here, on my local laptop?

00:07:58.630 --> 00:07:59.380
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah.

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:01.870
The Cloud Functions team
has covered here, too.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:03.550
For local development
and testing,

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:05.200
check out the
Functions Framework,

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:06.783
a set of open-source
libraries letting

00:08:06.783 --> 00:08:08.325
you create lightweight
functions that

00:08:08.325 --> 00:08:09.860
run in many different
environments,

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:12.550
including Cloud Functions,
locally on your laptop,

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:15.460
like you wanted, Cloud
Run, or other Knative-based

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:16.270
environments.

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:18.067
Check it out with
the links down below.

00:08:18.067 --> 00:08:19.150
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:22.000
Cloud Functions is quite
the versatile product.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.030
I'll definitely consider it
for its target use cases.

00:08:25.030 --> 00:08:27.670
And now are there any other
migrations that App Engine

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:29.320
developers should consider?

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:30.070
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah.

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:32.110
Most of the videos in
this "Migration Series"

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:34.720
involves migrating off of the
original App Engine-bundled

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:37.200
services, like switching
away from App Engine

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.240
NDB to either Cloud
NDB or Cloud Datastore,

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:42.549
and those are covered
in modules 2 and 3.

00:08:42.549 --> 00:08:44.230
Those with App
Engine push queues

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:46.300
can migrate those
to Cloud Tasks.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:49.330
This is covered in
modules 7, 8, and 9.

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:52.000
Modules 12 and 13 cover
moving off of App Engine

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.790
memcache to Cloud
Memory Store and Redis.

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:57.370
And we already discussed
containerizing App Engine

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:59.800
apps for Cloud Run
with or without Docker,

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:02.387
and those are featured
in modules 4 and 5.

00:09:02.387 --> 00:09:03.220
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:04.780
So many modules, Wes.

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:07.840
I'm not supposed to do each
module, one after another,

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:08.380
right?

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:09.100
WESLEY CHUN: That's right.

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:10.510
And more of them are coming.

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:11.980
Developers should
look at whichever

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.830
modules make the most sense
for their apps and use cases.

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:16.490
We're here whenever you need us.

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:17.950
Thanks for riding
with us on Porter

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:19.960
for all of these different
migration journeys.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:22.870
This is Wesley from Google Cloud
on behalf of Martin and Porter,

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:25.300
and we hope to see you at
the next "Migration Station"

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:27.460
or on another "Serverless
Expedition" soon.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:30.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

